#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Le Verrier's Paris meridian series of Mercury (1801–1828, 1836–1842), re-solved.

The 187 equations of condition below are the ones Le Verrier formed from ~400
meridian observations, as re-tabulated (with the δe′, δπ′ columns added) in
arXiv:1104.0548, "On the first determination of Mercury's perihelion advance"
(Table, §2). Each row: coefficients of δε, δn, δe0, δπ0, δe′, δπ′; the
observed-minus-computed APPARENT GEOCENTRIC LONGITUDE (″); the weight.

Why it is here (doc 13 §1.8, plan IP-mercury-anomaly-projection T3): the
question was whether Mercury's "missing advance" could be an equatorial
projection artefact of the observers' chain. These equations are formed in
ecliptic LONGITUDE — positions were converted from RA/Dec of date to longitude
before any rate was formed — so solving them shows what the longitude-only
meridian data say, independent of the transits and of any modern constant.

Result (weighted least squares, this script): δπ′ ≈ 0.43 ″/yr = 43 ″/cy
(paper: 42.8 ± 12.6; GR: 42.98). The excess is present in longitude-reduced
data alone; there is no RA step in this chain for a dα/dλ slope to act on.

    python3 tools/explore/leverrier-meridian-refit.py
"""
